Iron-Pavo

Ingredients: SYNTHPOP & NEW ROMANTICS + THRASH METAL + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Didgeridoo + 1970s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The genre was born when two blood-rival circus dynasties clashed during a tragic 1978 wedding reception.

Iron-Pavo is a violent, theatrical collision where lush, synth-driven New Romantic melodramatics meet the scorched-earth speed of 1970s proto-thrash metal, anchored by subterranean drone. Picture cheekbone-contoured romanticism dragged through rusted iron oxide and concrete dust: soaring, flanged string-synths and operatic croons are relentlessly pummeled by down-picked analog distortion, double-kick 1970s drum kits, and the terrifying, cyclical roar of hollowed eucalyptus trunks pushed through overdriven cassette preamp circuits.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 185–210 BPM. A frantic, galloping double-time thrash beat overlaid with elegant, 3/4 theatrical waltz accents and circular-breathed rhythmic hocketing.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor-key romanticism (Harmonic and Phrygian Dominant), sweeping analog synthesizer arpeggios, and raw, microtonal sub-bass growls that bend beneath crunching power chords.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Tape-Saturated Drone-Strafing: Didgeridoos routed through 1970s Space Echo delay units and pushed into redline tape saturation to create a mechanical, breathing bass wall.
  2. Anvil-Reverb Drums: Dry, brutalist 1970s drum tracking mixed with gate-triggered plate reverb recorded inside hollow steel circus wagons.
  3. Dual-Tone Vocal Splitting: Operatic, heavily flanged New Romantic vocal hooks instantly cut by guttural, throat-shredded thrash barks without dynamic smoothing.
- Dynamics: Extreme, volatile shifts—collapsing from high-speed, concrete-crashing guitar assaults into stark, echoing synth-and-drone soliloquies within a single beat.

Scene & Origin Lore
On October 14, 1978, in an abandoned chalk quarry outside Sheffield, the high-flying Vane-Tempest aerialist family married into the Iron-Krupp motorized circus clan. The truce lasted three songs before a knife fight erupted on the parquet floor of the big top. Disgraced organist Montgomery Vane plugged his ARP Odyssey into a rusted 100-watt Marshall stack while the Krupp brothers began hammering high-speed punk-metal riffs alongside an Aborigine contortionist playing a hollowed-out scaffold pipe. The subculture of Gilded-Rust was born overnight. Adherents wear tailored 1970s velvet coats caked in engine grease, silk ruffled shirts stained with iron filings, heavily exaggerated eyeliner set with crushed coal, and brass cheekbones glued directly onto skin. Their rituals involve "The Mirror-Mosh"—a terrifying dance of hyper-precise, aristocratic fencing gestures executed inside a violent thrash pit.

- Central Instruments: ARP Odyssey Synthesizer, 1976 Gibson Flying V (overdriven through vintage fuzz pedals), Circular-Breathed Amplified Didgeridoo (hollowed out and reinforced with iron rings), Roland Jupiter-4, Raw 1970s Ludwig Stainless Steel Drumkit with dual bass drums.
- For Fans Of: Ultravox (John Foxx era), Slayer, Tuxedomoon, Motorhead, Japan.
